!!id:"https://schemas.example.com/2026/39/orders/orders-api-1.tn"
!!import:"https://tson.io/2026/39/io/ltr8/http/meta-service.tn"
!!import:"https://schemas.example.com/2026/39/orders/orders-1.tn"
@doc:"""
  Both: the protocol of orders-1.tn, and the http projection that binds it. Every endpoint is a `!binding`
  naming an exchange and borrowing its signature. The exchange is a reference the resolver judges, a
  qualified name through the protocol's entry and its key -- `orders.place_order` -- so a misspelt exchange
  fails at load. What a binding adds is the placement of that exchange's one request record over the HTTP
  request, which is where the two views are allowed to look different:

    POST   /orders        new_order   { order  idempotency_key }  order unwrapped as the body,
                                                                  idempotency_key as Idempotency-Key
    GET    /orders/{id}   order_ref   { id }                      id from the path
    GET    /orders        order_query { status  page  page_size } all three in the query (a GET's remainder)
    DELETE /orders/{id}   order_ref   { id }                      id from the path; nothing left over

  `@binds:[orders]`, written before the projection's entry, is a claim the reader holds it to: every
  exchange of `orders` has a binding here, or an entry in `@not_bound` giving the reason it does not.
  """
!schema {
  @binds:[orders]
  orders_http => !http {
    "/orders" => {
      @summary:"Place an order."
      @doc:"""
        Accepts a new order and confirms it. Repeating the request with the same Idempotency-Key returns the
        original confirmation rather than placing a second order.
        """
      POST => !binding { exchange: orders.place_order  status: 201
                         body: order  headers: { idempotency_key => "Idempotency-Key" } }
      @summary:"Search orders."
      GET  => !binding { exchange: orders.list_orders }
    }
    "/orders/{id}" => {
      GET    => !binding { exchange: orders.get_order }
      DELETE => !binding { exchange: orders.cancel_order  status: 204 }
    }
  }
}
